// THE PER-ENTRY EMISSION INSTRUMENT.
//
// DESIGN's Building-&-checks section carries a declared rung drop titled "THE MEASUREMENT
// BANKRUPTCY DELETED A CAPABILITY, NOT ONLY ARTIFACTS". It states the temporary state plainly --
// no executable route to a per-entry emission board measurement exists, so a board figure quoted
// from the tree names nothing that can produce it -- and it names its own restoration trigger: a
// `.dag` entry point that emits, assembles and compiles ONE entry and returns the coded-diagnostic
// population, cited by name wherever a board figure is quoted. This module is that entry point.
// It is the INSTRUMENT, not a board: it produces the numbers and stores none of them.
//
// WHY ONE ENTRY AND NOT THE CORPUS. gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission refuses a whole-bundle
// compile on the default remote runner -- the declared demand exceeds the runner's budget, and
// before that refusal existed the same run was killed with no diagnostic, so anything reading its
// output for a count read a fabricated zero. That module's own scope note records the measured
// alternative: a scoped `--entry` compile FITS on the runner that killed the whole-corpus run,
// because its working set is one import closure rather than the whole tree.
//
// EXECUTION PROVENANCE IS THE CARRIER'S FIRST JOB, ahead of any count it reports. DESIGN's
// execution-provenance-loss failure mode is the exact defect this instrument exists to end: a
// count of zero and an unreached stage must not render identically. So `refused before the emitter
// ran` has no spelling in the same shape as `emitted with zero diagnostics` -- they are different
// variants of EmissionMeasurement, and every stage that can prevent a later stage from executing
// appears in the carrier by name. A consumer cannot read a population without having been told
// which stages produced it.
//
// AND SPLITTING THE STATES IS ONLY HALF OF IT. Naming the stages still lets two different compilers
// report the same-looking population, so every outcome that carries a population also carries an
// EmissionMeasurementSubject -- source revision, working-tree standing, and the digests of the two
// binaries actually invoked -- established before the emitter runs. An unattributable population has
// no representation here: the one arm without a subject is the one that took no measurement.
//
// IT IS NOT A GATE. Nothing here blocks a merge, nothing is enrolled in the required run, and the
// exit status reports whether the MEASUREMENT completed, never whether the entry was clean: an
// entry with a thousand diagnostics is a successful measurement. Gating on emission convergence is
// a separate decision with a separate argument.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SUBJECT: WHAT PRODUCED THIS MEASUREMENT.
//
// Distinguishing the phase states above is necessary and it is NOT provenance. DESIGN separates the
// two deliberately: state-space conflation is repaired by SPLITTING STATES, execution-provenance
// loss is repaired by BINDING AN EXECUTION RECEIPT to the value. A carrier that names its stages but
// not its compiler can report the same-looking population from two different compilers, which is
// the same defect one level down from the one the stage split closes.
//
// So every outcome that carries a population also carries the subject that produced it, and the
// subject is established BEFORE the emitter runs. A component that cannot be observed refuses the
// run rather than being recorded as absent -- an unobserved digest is not the digest of nothing.
// The identity vocabulary is REUSED, never re-coined: CommitSha from extdeps.git.inspect, Digest
// from extdeps.crypto.hash. Minting a parallel identity vocabulary inside the instrument built to
// enforce single authority would be the violation it exists to measure.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS AN IDENTITY QUESTION ANSWERED WITH A LOCATOR, and that is stated rather than
// discovered later. `entry` here is a repository-relative PATH, because the actuation surface is
// `gunbc compile --entry`, which takes a path and offers no identity-keyed alternative. So a rename
// of the entry file reads as a different subject even when the declaration it names is unchanged.
// That is a property of the actuation boundary, not a modelling choice made here, and any consumer
// keying measurements by declaration identity has to carry the identity-to-path mapping itself and
// treat it as a fact that can go stale.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CARRIED, named rather than silently omitted: the emitted candidate tree's own digest
// and the installed stage0 mirror's digest. Both are directory-wide readings whose cost is a walk
// per run, and neither is needed to answer "which compiler produced this population" -- the two
// binary digests answer that. They are the next things to bind if a consumer needs to join
// measurements across trees rather than across compilers.
type WorkingTreeStanding
  = WorkingTreeClean
  | WorkingTreeDirty

// The assembly step is a coproduct rather than a directory string so a second assembly mode becomes
// a variant here rather than an unlabelled path difference between two measurements.
type EmissionAssemblyMode
  = SeedLinkedAssembly { std_bridge_dir: String }

type EmissionSubjectRead
  = SubjectRead { subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject }
  | SubjectUnreadable { cause: String }

type EmissionMeasurementSubject {
  entry: String
  source_revision: CommitSha
  working_tree: WorkingTreeStanding
  compiler: Digest
  assembler: Digest
  assembly_mode: EmissionAssemblyMode
}

fn working_tree_standing_text(standing: WorkingTreeStanding) -> String {
  match standing {
    WorkingTreeClean => "clean"
    WorkingTreeDirty => "dirty (the measured bytes are not the revision's bytes)"
  }
}

fn emission_assembly_mode_text(mode: EmissionAssemblyMode) -> String {
  match mode {
    SeedLinkedAssembly { std_bridge_dir: d } => concat("seed-linked via ", d)
  }
}

// THE COUNT AND ITS EXTENT ARE RENDERED BY ONE EXPRESSION, so a reader cannot receive the number
// without the standing of the set it counts. A separate optional row would let the count travel
// alone, which is the whole failure: `emit_population 2` reads as "this entry has two blocking
// diagnostics" when emit may have returned before the check that would have found eight more.
fn emit_population_rows(ds: List<GunbcDiagnostic>, files: EmittedFileCount) -> List<String> {
  [
    concat(
      concat("emit_population\t", to_string(count(ds))),
      concat("\tblocking\t", to_string(emit_blocking_count(ds: ds)))
    ),
    concat("emit_population_extent\t", emit_population_extent_text(extent: emit_population_extent_of(files: files)))
  ]
}

fn emission_subject_rows(subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject) -> List<String> {
  [
    concat("subject\t", subject.entry),
    concat("source_revision\t", subject.source_revision as String),
    concat("working_tree\t", working_tree_standing_text(standing: subject.working_tree)),
    concat("compiler\t", subject.compiler.hex as String),
    concat("assembler\t", subject.assembler.hex as String),
    concat("assembly_mode\t", emission_assembly_mode_text(mode: subject.assembly_mode))
  ]
}

// The stages, in the order they run. A stage names a place the line can stop, which is why the
// list is a closed coproduct rather than a string: an unreachable-stage refusal must be as
// enumerable as a diagnostic.
type EmissionStage
  = StageSubject
  | StageWorkspace
  | StageBinaries
  | StageEmit
  | StageEmitDecode
  | StageAssemble
  | StageManifest
  | StageCargo
  | StageCargoDecode

fn emission_stage_name(stage: EmissionStage) -> String {
  match stage {
    StageSubject => "subject"
    StageWorkspace => "workspace"
    StageBinaries => "binaries"
    StageEmit => "emit"
    StageEmitDecode => "emit-decode"
    StageAssemble => "assemble"
    StageManifest => "manifest"
    StageCargo => "cargo"
    StageCargoDecode => "cargo-decode"
  }
}

// FOUR OUTCOMES, AND THE FIRST THREE ARE THE POINT.
//
// EmissionUnreached  -- a stage refused before the emitter produced anything. There is no
//                       population, and the carrier cannot pretend there is an empty one.
// EmissionEmitRefused-- the emitter RAN and refused. This is a real measurement: the emit-stage
//                       population is exactly what the caller wants, and it is carried here rather
//                       than discarded because a refusing entry is the interesting case.
// EmissionCargoUnreached -- the emitter completed and a later stage refused. The emit population is
//                       real; the rustc population was never produced and is therefore absent,
//                       not empty.
// EmissionMeasured   -- every stage ran. Both populations are observations.
type EmissionMeasurement
  = EmissionSubjectUnestablished {
      entry: String
      stage: EmissionStage
      cause: String
    }
  | EmissionUnreached {
      subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject
      stage: EmissionStage
      cause: String
    }
  | EmissionEmitRefused {
      subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject
      exit_code: Int
      emit_diagnostics: List<GunbcDiagnostic>
      emitted_files: EmittedFileCount
    }
  | EmissionCargoUnreached {
      subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject
      stage: EmissionStage
      cause: String
      emit_diagnostics: List<GunbcDiagnostic>
      emitted_files: EmittedFileCount
    }
  | EmissionMeasured {
      subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject
      emitted_files: EmittedFileCount
      emit_diagnostics: List<GunbcDiagnostic>
      cargo_clean: Bool
      cargo_diagnostics: List<RustcCodedDiagnostic>
      cargo_lines_scanned: Int
    }

// The measurement is COMPLETE whenever the instrument reached a verdict about the entry, which
// includes an entry the compiler refused. It is INCOMPLETE only where the instrument itself could
// not run. Conflating the two is how an instrument reports its own breakage as a finding about
// its subject.
fn emission_measurement_completed(m: EmissionMeasurement) -> Bool {
  match m {
    EmissionSubjectUnestablished { entry: _, stage: _, cause: _ } => false
    EmissionUnreached { subject: _, stage: _, cause: _ } => false
    EmissionCargoUnreached { subject: _, stage: _, cause: _, emit_diagnostics: _, emitted_files: _ } => false
    EmissionEmitRefused { subject: _, exit_code: _, emit_diagnostics: _, emitted_files: _ } => true
    EmissionMeasured { subject: _, emitted_files: _, emit_diagnostics: _, cargo_clean: _, cargo_diagnostics: _, cargo_lines_scanned: _ } => true
  }
}

fn emit_decode_unreached(subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject, read: EmitDiagnosticRead) -> EmissionMeasurement {
  EmissionUnreached {
    subject: subject,
    stage: StageEmitDecode,
    cause: emit_diagnostic_read_refusal_reason(read: read)
  }
}

// THE CARGO HALF, run only once the emit half has produced a real population. It is a separate
// function so that "the emitter completed" is established before anything downstream can be
// reported, and so the stage that refuses is named at the site that knows it.
//
// THE ASSEMBLE ARM CHECKS BOTH the assembler's exit status and the verdict marker it prints.
// Reading only the status would admit a future arm that exits 0 while refusing; reading only the
// marker would admit a crash that never printed anything.
//
// THE CARGO-DECODE ARM IS A REFUSAL, NOT A CLEAN BUILD. A message stream the decoder could not
// read carries the decoder's own located cause, so the reader learns which line stopped it rather
// than receiving a population that silently lost members. A stream that simply STOPS -- a killed
// build -- refuses there too, so a prefix never reaches this carrier as a population.
//
// AND THE CLEAN VERDICT COMES FROM CARGO'S OWN TERMINAL MESSAGE, not from the transport's exit
// status beside it: `build-finished` is emitted BY the run being measured, which is what makes it
// a fact about that run rather than about a process someone observed nearby.
fn measure_cargo_half(
  subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject,
  root: String,
  out_dir: String,
  cssl_assemble_bin: String,
  emit_diagnostics: List<GunbcDiagnostic>,
  emitted_files: EmittedFileCount
) -> EmissionMeasurement {
  let src_dir = shell.Mkdir.Parents(path: concat(out_dir, "/src"))
  if !src_dir.success {
    EmissionCargoUnreached {
      subject: subject,
      stage: StageAssemble,
      cause: concat("could not create the emitted crate's src directory under ", out_dir),
      emit_diagnostics: emit_diagnostics,
      emitted_files: emitted_files
    }
  } else {
    let assemble = gunbc.WitnessBin.Run(
      workdir: root,
      bin_path: cssl_assemble_bin,
      args: [
        "--out-dir", out_dir,
        "--entry-dag", subject.entry,
        "--root", root,
        "--std-bridge-dir", cssl_std_bridge_shims_rel
      ]
    )
    if !assemble.success || !string_contains(s: assemble.stdout, pattern: "CSSL_ASSEMBLE: PASS") {
      EmissionCargoUnreached {
        subject: subject,
        stage: StageAssemble,
        cause: concat("cssl_assemble refused: ", concat(assemble.stdout, assemble.stderr)),
        emit_diagnostics: emit_diagnostics,
        emitted_files: emitted_files
      }
    } else {
      let manifest = Filesystem.Write(
        path: concat(out_dir, "/Cargo.toml"),
        content: cssl_v1_compiled_probe_lib_cargo_toml(root: root)
      )
      if !manifest.success {
        EmissionCargoUnreached {
          subject: subject,
          stage: StageManifest,
          cause: concat("could not write the probe crate manifest under ", out_dir),
          emit_diagnostics: emit_diagnostics,
          emitted_files: emitted_files
        }
      } else {
        let build = cargo.Build.BuildManifestMessages(
          workdir: out_dir,
          manifest_path: concat(out_dir, "/Cargo.toml"),
          target_dir: concat(out_dir, "/target"),
          extra_args: ["--release", "--lib"]
        )
        match cargo_scan_diagnostics(stdout: build.stdout) {
          CargoDiagnosticsRead { diagnostics: ds, lines_scanned: n, build_succeeded: ok, terminal_seen: _ } =>
            EmissionMeasured {
              subject: subject,
              emitted_files: emitted_files,
              emit_diagnostics: emit_diagnostics,
              cargo_clean: ok,
              cargo_diagnostics: ds,
              cargo_lines_scanned: n
            }
          scan =>
            EmissionCargoUnreached {
              subject: subject,
              stage: StageCargoDecode,
              cause: concat(
                cargo_diagnostic_scan_refusal_reason(scan: scan),
                concat(" — cargo stderr: ", build.stderr)
              ),
              emit_diagnostics: emit_diagnostics,
              emitted_files: emitted_files
            }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// ONE ENTRY IN, ONE MEASUREMENT OUT. `entry` is a repository-relative `.dag` path, exactly what
// `gunbc compile --entry` takes.
//
// A COMPILER THAT RAN AND REFUSED IS A MEASUREMENT, not an instrument failure: the population it
// produced is the answer, so it travels with the refusal rather than being discarded for it.
// ESTABLISHING THE SUBJECT, and every component that cannot be observed REFUSES the run. There is
// deliberately no partial subject: a measurement whose compiler digest is "unavailable" is a
// population nobody can attribute, which is the state this receipt exists to make unrepresentable.
fn establish_subject(entry: String, root: String, gunbc_bin: String, cssl_assemble_bin: String) -> EmissionSubjectRead {
  let head = git.Inspect.HeadCommit()
  if !head.success {
    SubjectUnreadable { cause: "could not read the source revision (git rev-parse HEAD)" }
  } else {
    let status = git.Inspect.WorkingTreeStatus()
    if !status.success {
      SubjectUnreadable { cause: "could not read the working-tree standing (git status --porcelain)" }
    } else {
      match sha256sum_file_digest_via_shell(path: gunbc_bin as NonEmptyStr) {
        Sha256FileDigestUnavailable { path: p, reason: r } =>
          SubjectUnreadable { cause: concat(concat("could not digest the compiler at ", p as String), concat(": ", r)) }
        Sha256FileDigest { digest: compiler } =>
          match sha256sum_file_digest_via_shell(path: cssl_assemble_bin as NonEmptyStr) {
            Sha256FileDigestUnavailable { path: p, reason: r } =>
              SubjectUnreadable { cause: concat(concat("could not digest the assembler at ", p as String), concat(": ", r)) }
            Sha256FileDigest { digest: assembler } =>
              SubjectRead {
                subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject {
                  entry: entry,
                  source_revision: head.sha,
                  working_tree: if status.entries_nul == "" { WorkingTreeClean } else { WorkingTreeDirty },
                  compiler: compiler,
                  assembler: assembler,
                  assembly_mode: SeedLinkedAssembly { std_bridge_dir: cssl_std_bridge_shims_rel }
                }
              }
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn measure_entry_emission(entry: String) -> EmissionMeasurement {
  let root = git.Inspect.Toplevel()
  let out_dir = shell.Mktemp.Dir()
  if !out_dir.success {
    EmissionSubjectUnestablished {
      entry: entry,
      stage: StageWorkspace,
      cause: "could not create a temporary directory for the emitted crate"
    }
  } else {
    let gunbc_built = witness_bin_ready(root: root.path, bin: witness_bin(var_name: "GUNBC", bin_name: "gunbc"))
    let cssl_built = witness_bin_ready(root: root.path, bin: witness_bin(var_name: "CSSL_ASSEMBLE", bin_name: "cssl_assemble"))
    if !gunbc_built || !cssl_built {
      EmissionSubjectUnestablished {
        entry: entry,
        stage: StageBinaries,
        cause: "the gunbc and cssl_assemble release binaries are not both buildable — the instrument invokes the compiler it is measuring as a subprocess, so an absent binary is a refusal and never a clean measurement"
      }
    } else {
      let gunbc_bin = witness_bin_release_path(root: root.path, bin_name: "gunbc")
      let cssl_assemble_bin = witness_bin_release_path(root: root.path, bin_name: "cssl_assemble")
      match establish_subject(entry: entry, root: root.path, gunbc_bin: gunbc_bin, cssl_assemble_bin: cssl_assemble_bin) {
        SubjectUnreadable { cause: c } =>
          EmissionSubjectUnestablished { entry: entry, stage: StageSubject, cause: c }
        SubjectRead { subject: subject } =>
          measure_with_subject(
            subject: subject,
            root: root.path,
            out_dir: out_dir.path,
            gunbc_bin: gunbc_bin,
            cssl_assemble_bin: cssl_assemble_bin
          )
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE EMIT HALF, with the subject already bound. A compiler that RAN AND REFUSED is a measurement,
// not an instrument failure: the population it produced is the answer, so it travels with the
// refusal rather than being discarded for it.
fn measure_with_subject(
  subject: EmissionMeasurementSubject,
  root: String,
  out_dir: String,
  gunbc_bin: String,
  cssl_assemble_bin: String
) -> EmissionMeasurement {
  let emit = gunbc.WitnessBin.Run(
    workdir: root,
    bin_path: gunbc_bin,
    args: [
      "compile",
      "--source-root", "dag",
      "--source-root", "src/v2",
      "--entry", subject.entry,
      "--output-dir", out_dir,
      "--target", "rust",
      "--dependency-pool-index", "primary-precedence"
    ]
  )
  let read = read_emit_diagnostics(stderr: emit.stderr)
  match read {
    EmitDiagnosticsRead { diagnostics: ds, emitted_files: files, declared_total: _ } =>
      if !emit.success {
        EmissionEmitRefused {
          subject: subject,
          exit_code: emit.exit_code,
          emit_diagnostics: ds,
          emitted_files: files
        }
      } else {
        measure_cargo_half(
          subject: subject,
          root: root,
          out_dir: out_dir,
          cssl_assemble_bin: cssl_assemble_bin,
          emit_diagnostics: ds,
          emitted_files: files
        )
      }
    EmitSummaryAbsent => emit_decode_unreached(subject: subject, read: read)
    EmitSummaryUnreadable { line: _ } => emit_decode_unreached(subject: subject, read: read)
    EmitPopulationMismatch { decoded: _, declared: _ } => emit_decode_unreached(subject: subject, read: read)
    EmitSummaryDisagreement { compiled_total: _, severity_total: _ } => emit_decode_unreached(subject: subject, read: read)
    EmitScopeUnconfirmed => emit_decode_unreached(subject: subject, read: read)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RENDERING. Joinable rows, never a summary sentence.
//
// The header states the stage the run reached BEFORE any number appears, so a reader cannot take a
// count out of context: a report whose first line says the emitter never ran carries no counts at
// all, because there are none to carry.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn emission_report_rows(m: EmissionMeasurement) -> List<String> {
  match m {
    EmissionSubjectUnestablished { entry: e, stage: s, cause: c } => [
      concat("subject\t", e),
      concat("outcome\tsubject-unestablished\tstage\t", emission_stage_name(stage: s)),
      concat("cause\t", c),
      "provenance\t(none — the instrument could not say what would have produced a measurement, so it took none)"
    ]
    EmissionUnreached { subject: subject, stage: s, cause: c } =>
      concat(
        emission_subject_rows(subject: subject),
        [
          concat("outcome\tunreached\tstage\t", emission_stage_name(stage: s)),
          concat("cause\t", c)
        ]
      )
    EmissionEmitRefused { subject: subject, exit_code: code, emit_diagnostics: ds, emitted_files: files } =>
      concat(
        concat(
          emission_subject_rows(subject: subject),
          [
            concat("outcome\temit-refused\texit_code\t", to_string(code)),
            concat("emitted_files\t", emitted_file_count_text(files: files))
          ]
        ),
        concat(
          concat(
            emit_population_rows(ds: ds, files: files),
            ["cargo_population\t(not produced — the emitter refused, so no crate was assembled)"]
          ),
          map(ds, d => concat("emit\t", gunbc_diagnostic_row(d: d)))
        )
      )
    EmissionCargoUnreached { subject: subject, stage: s, cause: c, emit_diagnostics: ds, emitted_files: files } =>
      concat(
        concat(
          emission_subject_rows(subject: subject),
          [
            concat("outcome\tcargo-unreached\tstage\t", emission_stage_name(stage: s)),
            concat("cause\t", c),
            concat("emitted_files\t", emitted_file_count_text(files: files))
          ]
        ),
        concat(
          concat(
            emit_population_rows(ds: ds, files: files),
            ["cargo_population\t(not produced)"]
          ),
          map(ds, d => concat("emit\t", gunbc_diagnostic_row(d: d)))
        )
      )
    EmissionMeasured {
      subject: subject,
      emitted_files: files,
      emit_diagnostics: ds,
      cargo_clean: clean,
      cargo_diagnostics: rs,
      cargo_lines_scanned: lines
    } =>
      concat(
        concat(
          concat(
            emission_subject_rows(subject: subject),
            [
              concat("outcome\tmeasured\tcargo_clean\t", to_string(clean)),
              concat("emitted_files\t", emitted_file_count_text(files: files))
            ]
          ),
          concat(
            emit_population_rows(ds: ds, files: files),
            [
              concat(
                concat("cargo_population\t", to_string(count(rs))),
                concat("\tcargo_message_lines\t", to_string(lines))
              )
            ]
          )
        ),
        concat(
          map(ds, d => concat("emit\t", gunbc_diagnostic_row(d: d))),
          map(rs, r => concat("cargo\t", rustc_diagnostic_row(d: r)))
        )
      )
  }
}

fn emission_report_text(m: EmissionMeasurement) -> String {
  concat(join(emission_report_rows(m: m), "\n"), "\n")
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CLI SURFACE.
//
//   gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 \
//     --entry dag/tools/emission_entry_instrument.dag --function measure \
//     --arg entry=dag/extdeps/cpu/ampere.dag --arg report=/tmp/board.tsv
//
// The report is WRITTEN rather than returned because `gunbc run` maps a returned value to an exit
// status and has no value-printing verb; the same wall tools.self_host_curated_probe_cargo records.
// The typed measurement is what measure_entry_emission returns -- this wrapper only renders it.
//
// THE EXIT STATUS REPORTS THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE SUBJECT. A completed measurement exits zero even
// when the entry it measured is full of errors, and an instrument that could not run exits nonzero
// even though it found nothing wrong with anything. Any other mapping would make a broken
// instrument indistinguishable from a clean subject, which is the failure this whole module is a
// response to.
fn measure(entry: String, report: String) -> ProcessExit {
  if entry == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "measure: --arg entry=<repo-relative .dag path> is required")
  } else {
    if report == "" {
      exit_failure(reason: "measure: --arg report=<path> is required — the measurement is written, not printed")
    } else {
      let m = measure_entry_emission(entry: entry)
      let write = Filesystem.Write(path: report, content: emission_report_text(m: m))
      if !write.success {
        exit_failure(reason: concat("measure: could not write the report to ", report))
      } else {
        if emission_measurement_completed(m: m) {
          ExitSuccess
        } else {
          exit_failure(
            reason: concat(
              concat("measure: the instrument did not complete for ", entry),
              concat(" — the report at ", concat(report, " names the stage that stopped it"))
            )
          )
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
